From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: drop bench profile A

Profile A was a bench switch: for a bounded time it set the chip-wide
ingress TPIDs to the rtl8_4 head tag (S 0x8899, C 0x0000), made the CPU
port re-emit both tags unmodified and gave the conduit the core parser
role, so that uploads from the switch could hit. It served its purpose
(the PPPoE upload encapsulation was measured with it) and is not for
service: with the ingress C-TPID at 0x0000 no 802.1Q tag is parsed, so
the tagged WAN's downloads fall back to software.

Uploads from the switch are to be classified by the S-R mode instead,
which leaves the ingress TPIDs alone. Remove profile A ahead of it:

- its apply, revert and timer, the register snapshot it kept apart, its
  generation counter and the -EAGAIN check on it under the lock;
- its gates in ppe_offload_flow_replace(): ingress through a DSA conduit
  is again always declined as unparsed, and the MY_MAC and tagged-ingress
  checks that only applied under A go;
- ppe_offload_tpids_moved() and its use in edma_port_feature_check():
  the parser TPIDs are now never moved, so an 802.1Q tag is always
  followed;
- the write side of debugfs offload_profile, which becomes read only
  (0400) and prints off, plain or E; the profile_a field of
  offload_flows' header line.

The PPPoE upload encapsulation stays. It is gated on the ingress being
an S-R identity (ingress_sr), which nothing sets yet, so PPPoE uploads
are declined as before outside the bench. The parser register
definitions in ppe_regs.h stay for the S-R mode.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
